  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes signaling pathways and treatment targets in HER2-positive breast cancer. It centers on the HER family at the cell membrane, showing EGFR, HER2, HER3, and HER4 forming dimers such as HER2–EGFR, HER2–HER3, HER2–HER4, and HER2 homodimers. These activated receptors feed into two major downstream cascades: the RAS–RAF–MEK–ERK pathway and the PI3K–AKT–mTOR pathway, both converging on gene expression, cell-cycle progression, and ultimately cell survival and proliferation.\n\nAround these pathways, the diagram maps current drug classes. Antibodies and antibody–drug conjugates are positioned at the receptor level, including trastuzumab, pertuzumab, T-DM1, and T-DXd, while tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as lapatinib, neratinib, pyrotinib, and tucatinib appear across multiple HER2-containing dimers. Further downstream, MEK/ERK inhibitors and PI3K–AKT–mTOR inhibitors are indicated. The figure also links estrogen receptor signaling to the cell-cycle machinery, with endocrine therapy and CDK4/6 inhibitors acting through cyclin D1, CDK4/6, Rb, and related regulators.\n\nOn the right, immune evasion is included through the PD-1/PD-L1 axis between a T cell and tumor cell, with immune checkpoint inhibitors shown as another therapeutic strategy. Overall, the image presents an integrated treatment map spanning receptor blockade, intracellular signaling inhibition, endocrine therapy, cell-cycle control, and immunotherapy.",
